rd J3, I keep W4 and W5 which can form a chow with W3 or W6. That seems promising because W3 and W6 are available (1 each). If I discard W4, I have W5 and J3, which are not connected, so I would need to draw a pair for one of them and then a meld for the other, harder. If I discard W5, similar issue with W4 and J3. So discarding J3 seems best to pursue a chow with W4,W5. Also, by discarding J3, I move towards \"All Simples\" (no honors) which is 2 points. My melds are all simples, so if my final hand has no honors, I get All Simples (2 points). Also I have three chows already, so if I get another chow, I could have \"All Chows\" (2 points) if my pair is also a suited tile (T5 pair is suited). So potential points: All Simples (2), All Chows (2), maybe Short Straight? Possibly other patterns. But I need at least 8 points total. With All Simples (2) and All Chows (2) that's only 4. I need more. Maybe \"Mixed Triple Chow\"? No, my chows are in different suits? I have two W chows and one T chow. Not identical. \"Pure Straight\"? I have chows in W: W2-W4 and W6-W8, not consecutive. \"Half Flush\"? I have only W and T suits, no honors if I discard J3, so not half flush. \"Full Flush\"? All same suit? No, I have T chow. So likely low scoring. But I also have the possibility of \"Last Tile Draw\" or \"Self Drawn\" etc. But I need to ensure my hand can reach 8 points. Actually, in Chinese Standard Mahjong, a winning hand must be worth at least 8 points. If my hand is too low, I cannot win even if I complete it. So I must aim for a hand that can score at least 8. Currently, with three chows, if I get a fourth chow and a pair, I can have All Chows (2) + All Simples (2) = 4. Plus maybe \"Short Straight\" if I have two consecutive chows in the same suit? I have W2-W4 and W6-W8, they are not consecutive (gap W5). So not short straight. \"Two Terminal Chows\"? No. \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\"? No. \"Edge Wait\" or \"Closed Wait\"? Possibly. \"Self Drawn\" (1). That still might n